Readable per-request KV transfer logging: one INFO line per tier movement.

The offloading connector already emits plenty of DEBUG detail, but all of it
is written for tracing one mechanism at a time -- chunk indices, job ids,
per-group skip decisions. None of it answers the question you actually have
while watching a live server: did this request's context come off the disk,
and how much of it moved? Answering that today means grepping several
different DEBUG lines across two modules and correlating them by hand.

This adds vllm/v1/kv_offload/transfer_log.py and calls it from the four
points where blocks actually change tier, producing lines like:

    KV LOAD  chatcmpl-a9b273614cd4c815-99de3654 | fs   -> RAM  |  44 blk
    KV LOAD  chatcmpl-a9b273614cd4c815-99de3654 | RAM  -> VRAM |  44 blk /  36608 tok (GPU had 50752 tok)
    KV STORE chatcmpl-b88083b7827488ae | VRAM -> RAM  |  12 blk /   9984 tok
    KV STORE chatcmpl-b88083b7827488ae | RAM  -> fs   |  12 blk

Both directions are two-legged because that is what the tiering manager
really does: secondary tiers cannot touch GPU memory, so a disk-backed
resume is promoted fs -> RAM first and only then pulled RAM -> VRAM. Each
leg is logged where it happens, so the pair reads top to bottom.

Placement:
  - tiering/manager.py _flush_pending_promotions()  -> "<tier> -> RAM"
  - tiering/manager.py complete_store() cascade     -> "RAM -> <tier>"
  - offloading/scheduler.py _lookup()               -> "RAM -> VRAM"
  - offloading/scheduler.py _build_store_jobs()     -> "VRAM -> RAM"

INFO, and on by default: the point is to sit next to the request log without
switching all of vLLM to DEBUG, which buries it. VLLM_KV_TRANSFER_LOG=0
silences it. Read once at import rather than per block -- this sits in a
per-transfer path.

Two details worth knowing when reading the output:

  - The connector caches the chunk size in __init__ as _log_chunk_tokens
    instead of reusing the loop-local tokens_per_chunk near the _lookup log
    site. That name is only bound inside the per-group branches, so touching
    it on a path that skipped them (no sliding-window groups, nothing in
    flight) would raise UnboundLocalError -- a logging statement must not be
    able to fail the request.

  - On the STORE line the block and token columns do not divide into each
    other. keys_to_store spans all four KV-cache groups (3 mamba + 1 full
    attention), so the block column counts per-group blocks while the token
    column is the request-level span they cover.

